diagnosis_text:
Campus 1 connectivity is currently healthy. End-to-end reachability from multiple non‑Campus subnets to Campus 1 hosts is confirmed: e.g., 10.5.0.x and 10.6.0.x hosts can ping 172.16.10.5/20.9/30.12/30.14 with 0% loss and low RTTs. On the “edge” device router16, both uplinks are operational: bond0 (to the Campus aggregation) is UP with eth0/eth1 as slaves, and eth2 (172.30.1.1/24 toward the core) is UP. OSPF is converged on router16 with neighbors in Full state on both sides: neighbor 10.2.2.2 on bond0 and neighbor 172.30.1.2 on eth2. The routing table on router16 contains OSPF-learned routes for all Campus 1 VLANs (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 172.16.30.0/24) via 10.2.2.2, and core routes via 172.30.1.2, matching the intended design. 

Mechanistically, if router16 had failed, we would see OSPF neighbors down and loss of those routes; instead, adjacencies are stable and traffic passes. The brief “frr.service inactive (dead)” report from systemd is misleading in this environment; frr is clearly running (vtysh shows live OSPF neighbors and OSPF-installed routes). 

Root cause: No active fault found; the morning outage was likely transient and self‑cleared or a misobservation. 

Corrective fix: None required now. Recommend enabling monitoring to alert on OSPF neighbor down events on router16 and reconciling FRR’s systemd unit so service state reflects reality. === END ===